{
  "dom_id": "step-q253859",
  "name": "Question 17",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14162617/assets/372aa80e15465694347dce5325537a10be1217e83050d609dfeec0347bad1116.png)\n\nWhat is the infimum of the function $y=f(x)$ shown above?\n\n${inf}_{x\\in R}f(x)=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "The infimum of a function is the infimum of the range of the function.\n\nIn this case, the range of $f$ is $A=[-2,2].$\n\nTo find the infimum of a set $A,$ we must find the set containing **all** lower bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of lower bounds) and take the maximum of this set.\n\nSo, in our case, we have that\n\n- any number $y\\le -2$ is a lower bound of $A,$\n\n- any number $y>-2$ is **not** a lower bound of $A$ because $-2,$ an element of $A,$ is smaller than $y.$\n\nTherefore, $(-\\infty ,-2]$ is the set of lower bounds of $A,$ and\n\n$$\n{inf}_{x\\in R}f(x)=inf(A)=max(-\\infty ,-2]=-2.\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
